  "notes": [
    "Unlike most files in this corpus, this source is a single self-contained calendar-year 2026 cycle (January 4 through December 27) -- no cross-liturgical-year boundary to reconcile, and no future -2025.json is implied.",
    "Sunday, May 31, 2026 has no entry at all in the source (the calendar jumps from May 24 directly to June 7) -- confirmed by rendering the actual page images, not a transcription gap. Nothing is fabricated to fill it.",
    "The source visually distinguishes most parish/congregation entries (teal/blue text) from diocesan administrative or ministry entries (black text), which this file's own parish-vs-category classification follows in most cases -- but content, not color alone, decided borderline cases: 'Trinity Center' (June 21) is East Carolina's own diocesan retreat/conference center, not a congregation, and is recorded as a `category` subject despite being printed in the same teal color as every parish name, since no place is given and it names a facility rather than a congregation.",
    "September 13's 'Grace Episcopal & Christ the King Lutheran Church, Whiteville' is a shared/ecumenical building (an Episcopal and a Lutheran congregation sharing one facility) -- preserved as one combined parish name rather than split, since the source prints it as a single line.",
    "October 18's 'St. Lukes Episcopal Mission' (no apostrophe) is preserved exactly as printed, per this corpus's standing rule against silently correcting apparent typos.",
    "May 10's entry names the Presiding Bishop and President of the House of Deputies together with 'Staff of The Episcopal Church' as one combined line in the source; kept as a single category subject rather than split, since the source gives no clear boundary between the two officers' names and the trailing 'and Staff' clause."
